uit is  all you need  you know i've i've argued for ages it's  just an environmental policy look if you  encourage people to put their tumble  dryers and washing machines on later at  night  okay um that will actually reduce carbon  emissions simply because the uk is kind  of nuclear powered or powered heavily by  renewables  at 10 o'clock in the evening in a way  that it isn't at five o'clock  now if you try to make that compulsory  or you use the price mechanism  what happens to people who work the  night shift they can't do it  you don't want them leaving their house  with a tumble dryer on what happens to  people who live in a flat  and their washing machine is immediately  above their neighbor's bedroom  which means it's going to hit the spin  cycle at one o'clock in the morning  those people can't do it now the great  thing with persuasion is if you've got a  good reason  you can advance that reason and you're  now immune  or freed from the obligation the second  you start using economic incentives or  legal uh  legal directives that nuance gets  completely lost  and you try and design legislation for  everybody which in some cases ends up  working for nobody  and we've only really seen that  persuasion you know you've had people  like gareth southgate  making a short video asking young people  to get vaccinated but  i've not really heard anything else  really in that persuasion side  on the other hand we have heard about  vaccine passports being coming in for  nightclubs under the thought that under  30s go to nightclubs and  this is what's going to get them to get  get jabbed what do you make of that we  had some  so mark warport who is um patrick  valenz's predecessor in the chief  scientific advisor role  on spectator tv recently and he was  calling that a nudge  i mean it feels more like a shelf yes  the vaccine passport of course it's  it's it's ambiguous in a way in that  you know i i mean the way i describe  myself is a is a default libertarian i  don't  i don't accept the idea that there  aren't coordination problems i don't  accept the idea that there isn't a need  for  uh central uh um i i don't object you  know to put it very simply i don't  object to people painting lines in the  car park  okay you know there are certain you know  you know if you're an extreme  libertarian you'd say it's interfering  with my right to park at the diagonal  i don't object to that kind of thing you  know  painting white lines on the road is a  nudge if you like  okay and you know even deirdre mccloskey  who's more libertarian i think than i am  argues that there are there's a large  informational role that government can  play  which needn't involve any kind of  compulsion at all now  where does the issuing of a passport  where it's up to the private sector to  determine whether they require that  passport or not  where does that sit now to me  personally and i admit this is very very  subjective to me that's relatively  uncontroversial if the passport is being  policed by private entities  who simply say sorry it's our restaurant  if you're not prepared to do this  you can't come in when it becomes  employers for example or academic  institutions you know  novak no no vaxx no lecture that gets a  bit more contentious  because you can argue well you don't  necessarily have free choice there  you know you don't you can't suddenly  switch university according to their  vaccine policy and so it's complicated i  generally regard the imposition of  vaccine rules um  by private institutions as broadly  speaking acceptable  it gets more contentious um if you like  when it it starts to be imposed in areas  where  i would i would argue that um  mark squaring you know i i don't really  understand the massive  descent over masks because to me you  know there are plenty of inconvenient  things like wearing underpants that i do  really you know not you know not  necessarily for my own self-interest but  simply to avoid alarming other people  and i would i would place mask wearing  in the field of courtesy  which is even if you don't believe in  them and i think they do work by the way  you know i think at some level they  undoubtedly work  but even if you don't believe in them  some people do  and it's deeply you know discourteous to  elderly people who may be alarmed by  someone  uh wandering around the shops without a  mask i think it's simply an act of  courtesy  to other people and you know it also  sits within i think you know the  reasonable realm of  um the precautionary principle you know  well this might work and generally the  cost-benefit  uh thing looks fairly high when you're  demanding people  um are vaccinated who are young  for example and there are side effects  to the vaccine  among the young you know they're you  know they're not particularly unpleasant  for the most part  but they're not negligible i'm getting a  bit more uncomfortable there and i might  also raise the question well  what if to be honest it isn't necessary  to hit 100  and rory just on that vaccine passport  point some people have been saying that  because it's coming in in september  not immediately that actually all of it  is just a ploy for people to get  vaccinated and come september  these passports may not even come into  place  but what i wonder about that is that  isn't it quite a hit on the government's  credibility  to do that kind of u-turn to kind of  dangle this kind of threat over young  people and then rescinded as if they  never wanted to do it in the first place  i mean  that credibility hit surely in the minds  of people is actually not insignificant  well it's a very interesting question  isn't it which is how should we react  to legislation which is  really exists for its signaling value  rather than  as an actual restraint on behavior  so it acts as an accelerant it  encourages people to act fast it's  equivalent to  only four seats left at this price which  is you might as well do it now because  you'll have to do it ultimately  now as you might expect working in the  advertising industry with a strong  interest in kind of behavioral science  um legislation that i mean actually  quite a lot of legislation in a way  works that way  i mean we don't impose speed limits uh  rigorously okay we you know we we very  rarely find people for going  um 73 miles an hour and so legislation  which  uh which which exists in potential but  isn't necessarily  uh enforced can have a value because it  essentially  it you know it might accelerate a  behavior it might um  it may might make corporate decision  making easier because you can say this  is going to be a legal requirement in  five years time  so we might as well start acting now all  our competitors will be doing this  so in solving coordination problems um  laws which act to send a message  rather than to actually necessarily be  enforced i would argue  actually from a libertarian point of  view fairly desirable  in a funny kind of way because they  solve the coordination problem  without necessarily limiting liberty  and so the way i always phrase it is i'm  not a pure libertarian but i'm glad such  people exist  because i think that mentality uh plays  an essential component  part in looking at decision-making  over how government intervenes with the  populace  i'm i'm not massively sympathetic  towards anti-masters because it seems to  be a very very small price to pay  and it seems to be a slightly deranged  fear anti-vaxxers  i would seek if possible because you  know there are potential arguments  against universal vaccination  um and anti-vaxxers in that case i would  seek to find an accommodation with them  rory sutherland thanks for joining  spectator tv  [Music]  you


